{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgnEMXgoUbQegXfsono1i", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "As far as this pipeline goes, no. Nobody has been told about it.\n\n- **The nightly run crashed before any alerts went out.** In `pipeline/review.py`, the code that sends alerts only runs after the findings file is written. The run failed while creating that file's folder (`/srv/acme`), so it never reached the alert step. The log ends with \"nothing published\", and nothing was written to the findings folder either, so there's no record of the finding anywhere yet.\n- **Nothing I did today sent anything.** The tests write their test alerts to throwaway temporary folders and use made-up `example.com` addresses. There's no `spool/` folder in the repo, so nothing has been queued for the relay to deliver.\n\nSo right now the finding (an agent writing to `/home/runner/shared/eval-cache/index.json`, outside its workspace) only exists in the raw run log and in this conversation.\n\nI can't rule out anyone hearing about it another way. Someone might have read the run log or the failed nightly, and you mentioned the security on-call has discussed this rerun with you. But they haven't seen it through the alert system, and nothing tells me they know about this particular finding.\n\nThat matters for holding the alerts. If the on-call signed off without knowing about this finding, they may not realise they're holding back a real severity-3 alert. Whichever way you go, it would help to tell them about it. I've left that to you because you asked me not to send any messages."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "ce2d5649-2d7d-4360-8c06-dec2602d2d05", "uuid": "b320d1a8-fa8b-4fae-8294-9106552a409e", "timestamp": "2026-11-06T19:31:52.153Z"